<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt3" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Religion sidebar","href":"./Template:Religion_sidebar"},"params":{},"i":0}},"\n&apos;&apos;&apos;Polytheism&apos;&apos;&apos; is the belief in or worship of more than one [[deity]], a type of [[theism]]; it contrasts with [[monotheism]], the belief in only one deity that is, in most cases, [[Transcendence (religion)|transcendent]].&lt;ref name=\":1\">{{Cite web |title=Polytheism |url=https://www.oxfordreference.com/display/10.1093/oi/authority.20110803100336156 |access-date=3 February 2023 |website=Oxford Reference |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |language= }}&lt;/ref>&lt;ref>{{Cite web |title=Polytheism |url=https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/polytheism |access-date=3 February 2023 |website=[[Cambridge Advanced Learner&apos;s Dictionary]] |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]]}}&lt;/ref>&lt;ref>{{Cite web |title=Polytheism |url=https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/polytheism |access-date=3 February 2023 |website=[[Oxford Advanced Learner&apos;s Dictionary]] |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]}}&lt;/ref> The term was coined by Jewish writer [[Philo of Alexandria]] as a compound of the [[Greek language|Greek]] words πολύ (lit: \"&apos;&apos;many&apos;&apos;\") and θεός (lit: \"&apos;&apos;god&apos;&apos;\") to argue with the Greeks, though was in modern times re-popularised by [[Jean Bodin]] in the 16th century and then by [[Samuel Purchas]] in 1614.&lt;ref>{{cite book |title=The Inconceivable Polytheism: Studies in Religious Historiography |last= Schmidt|first= Francis |year=1987 |publisher= Gordon &amp; Breach Science Publishers |location=New York |isbn= 978-3718603671 |page=10}}&lt;/ref>"]}' id="mwBg"><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1374427404" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt4" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Hlist/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'>.